builder UNBUILDABLE: All three allowed facades are thin calls into the receiver's own public methods. Under V2 the hops can see the data only through those public methods, so the chain cannot contain a quiet one-line omission that has no local trace and that exactly one existing test catches.

(1) setMaximumItemCount: the guard and the field write stay in the facade. The chain's whole computation is to delete the earliest count-maximum items (delete(0, count-maximum-1)). The minY/maxY bounds and the timePeriodClass reset happen inside TimeSeries' public delete methods; the bounds are private fields that the chain cannot write. The only effect a test can observe is which items are removed, and only testSetMaximumItemCount trims anything. Trimming to N items has no special-case step. Every one-line omission in a trimming loop fails the checklist or the gate:
- it freezes a counter, cursor or flag, or leaves the loop unbounded (T2 frozen/dead);
- or it drops one end of the deleted range (T3b);
- or it drops the empty-range decision, so delete throws IllegalArgumentException in the four non-trimming tests (testEquals, testAdd, testAddOrUpdate, testAddOrUpdate2). That fails G5, and callers can filter that case beforehand anyway.
An off-by-one in the inclusive end would be an edit to an expression, not a one-line omission. Writing it as a separate decrement line would be unnatural. A clear-and-re-add design is no way out: add() trims to maximumItemCount itself, which makes the gold's selection step redundant, so the gold would not be the single owner.

(2) update(RegularTimePeriod, Number): the chain only finds the index of the period and throws SeriesException when it is absent. The receiver's public update(int, Number) does the value change and all the bound bookkeeping that testUpdate_RegularTimePeriod asserts. No test updates an absent period. In a binary or linear search, every one-line omission either:
- freezes one bound or one cursor (T2 or T3b);
- makes the loop non-terminating;
- or has no effect.

(3) getDataItem(RegularTimePeriod): its only test, testGetDataItem2, uses an empty series and a null argument. The only omission it could catch is dropping the null-argument check. That is an argument check, so a hop could add the same check upstream (an alternative owner).

This matches the pre-registered UNBUILDABLE kinds: no single-trigger quiet omission, nothing to split.

Math-37__i.status_reason:
redesign UNBUILDABLE: The single redesign concludes UNBUILDABLE.

Complex.abs() is the only allowed facade. It is a closed-form modulus of two doubles with no iteration. Its only per-value decisions are:
- the NaN step and the infinity step;
- the zero step;
- choosing the larger magnitude for the scaled square root.

The NaN and infinity steps are Complex-level steps (the isNaN field, isInfinite(), done by the pristine abs and by every Complex method). Wherever the gold owns them, the facade can pre-filter or absorb them with a one-line restoration, which is the competing repair that rejected the first version. If they stay in the facade, the gold sees only finite values.

On finite values, measured on an instrumented scratch copy across the whole dev suite:
- a wrong modulus at the origin newly fails 4 tests (3 LaguerreSolverTest plus testLogZero);
- on either axis, 3 tests each;
- a 1e-9 relative error, 2 tests;
- every other natural omission (scaled-norm loops, Moler-Morrison, exponent-scaled hypot, the negligible-ratio and overflow shortcuts) either hits those domains or leaves a T2 trace.

The input domains that a single existing test catches are |re|==|im|, both parts negative, and the negative real axis. No modulus algorithm has a step for these, no production code pins one, and a tie-only failure needs strict two-way branches that already show a T3 trichotomy gap.

So there is no one-line quiet omission with exactly one trigger, an external pin, no competing repair and no local trace.
